# Rule: SwiftUI Toolbars / Commands / Menus

Sources

- SwiftUI — Toolbars
- SwiftUI — Menus and commands

Scope

Applies to command surfaces in SwiftUI:

- toolbars
- menu-bar commands
- in-view menus
- command groups
- keyboard shortcut exposure
- toolbar customization-related command structure

This rule governs how actions are distributed across visible command surfaces
so that the app remains consistent, discoverable, and platform-appropriate.

Intent

Build command architecture that is:

- predictable
- discoverable
- scoped correctly
- platform-appropriate
- duplication-aware
- easy to maintain

Toolbars should expose the most important frequent actions.
Menus and commands should expose the full action model in an organized way.
In-view menus should provide compact, contextual access where space is limited.

Do not treat all action surfaces as interchangeable.

Core Principle

SwiftUI command surfaces have different jobs.

Toolbars:

- foreground the most common, high-value actions
- support immediate interaction
- should stay concise and intentional

Commands / menu bar:

- represent the broader action model of the app or scene
- provide discoverability and keyboard access
- should be structurally organized

Menus:

- provide space-efficient, context-dependent access
- should group related actions without cluttering the main interface

## Enforcements

- Use toolbars for a curated subset of the most common actions.
- Use scene commands for the broader action model, especially on macOS.
- Use in-view menus when context or space efficiency is the reason.
- Keep scene-level commands and visible toolbar actions semantically aligned.
- Do not overload toolbars with low-frequency or destructive actions by default.
- Do not hide essential app actions exclusively inside toolbar-only UI.
